{%- comment -%}
  One extra nav item defined by the theme rather than the Shopify menu.

  Navigation menus are store data, so the theme cannot add an item to the Main
  menu — but it can render one of its own beside it. That is what this is, and
  it is why the Journal appears in the nav with no admin step.

  The URL resolves to a blog that actually EXISTS rather than a hard-coded path:
  a fresh Shopify store ships a "News" blog, and whichever blog is present is a
  better target than a handle that may not have been created yet. The configured
  URL is only used when the store has no blog at all.

  Rendered by both header-dropdown-menu and header-drawer so the desktop nav and
  the mobile menu can never disagree about what is in the navigation.

  Accepts:
    label       {string}  link text; nothing renders when blank
    url         {string}  fallback URL, used only if the store has no blog
    item_class  {string}  the host menu's own item class, so this is
                          indistinguishable from a real menu entry
    prefer_blog {boolean} resolve to the store's blog (default true)
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign hp_nav_url = url

  unless prefer_blog == false
    # Prefer the handle the article script creates, then fall back to whatever
    # blog the store does have, so the link is never a dead end.
    assign hp_blog = blogs['journal']
    if hp_blog.handle == blank
      for b in blogs
        assign hp_blog = b
        break
      endfor
    endif
    if hp_blog.handle != blank
      assign hp_nav_url = hp_blog.url
    endif
  endunless
-%}

{%- if label != blank and hp_nav_url != blank -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign hp_nav_active = false
    if request.path contains hp_nav_url
      assign hp_nav_active = true
    endif
  -%}
  <li>
    <a
      href="{{ hp_nav_url }}"
      class="{{ item_class }}"
      {% if hp_nav_active %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
    >
      <span{% if hp_nav_active %} class="header__active-menu-item"{% endif %}>
        {{- label | escape -}}
      </span>
    </a>
  </li>
{%- endif -%}
